WHAT A RECIPE CLAIMS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

A recipe is a change your own agent makes to your own repository, on a
branch, reviewed by you. It ends by handing back one line saying WHERE
something now lives, with a date. It never hands back a value, a score,
or a verdict.

- A recipe makes a check green. It does not make an application correct.
  A route can gain a ceiling and still do the wrong thing for the wrong
  person; nothing in this directory can close that gap. Your review does.
- Free, forever. Every recipe in this directory is free, and nothing here
  is held back or degraded to make something else worth paying for.
  Recipes sold by the same author live elsewhere and are never mixed in.
- Heuristic, and says so. A recipe reads what it can read and refuses what
  it cannot. It answers UNKNOWN rather than guess, and when it cannot
  finish it writes nothing at all.
- The builder, never the app. What a recipe records is that a person's
  agent applied it on a date. No claim like "protected by SkillBoss" or
  "SkillBoss verified" about an application may be derived from it.
- No telemetry. As published, no recipe here makes a network request or
  sends anything anywhere, with one stated exception: a recipe that must
  resolve a value at an outside source it names in its own contract, such
  as a commit reference for a tag. When that source cannot be reached, the
  recipe refuses and hands back the command, and changes nothing.
- Never less buildable. A recipe runs your project's own check before and
  after, and if the project was clean and is not, it puts every file back
  and refuses.

